/* data/learn-c/mth-12-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/mth-12-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"A statement is a sentence that is either true or false, but not both. Its truth value is T or F. Questions, commands and exclamations are not statements, and neither are open sentences whose truth depends on an unknown, such as x + 3 = 5.","w":["A sentence like 'x + 2 = 7' is not a statement until x is given a value."],"x":["A statement is a sentence that is either true or false, but not both. Its truth value is T (true) or F (false). 'The Earth moves around the Sun' is a statement with value T. '5 is an even number' is a statement with value F.","These are not statements: questions ('Is it raining?'), commands ('Close the door'), wishes and exclamations, and open sentences such as 'x + 3 = 5', whose truth depends on the value of x. A sentence with a variable becomes a statement only when the variable is given a value, or when a quantifier such as 'for all x' is added.","Simple statements are written with letters p, q, r. Common mistake: calling a sentence a statement because we feel that we know the answer. The test is only this: can it be called true or false right now?"],"e":"Which are statements? (a) The sum of 2 and 3 is 6. (b) What a lovely day! (c) x is greater than 4. Only (a) is a statement, and its value is F."},"1.2":{"s":"Simple statements are joined into compound statements by the connectives and (conjunction), or (disjunction), if-then (implication) and if and only if (biconditional). A conjunction is true only when both parts are true; a disjunction is false only when both parts are false. An implication is false only when the first part is true and the second is false.","w":["'If p then q' is TRUE whenever p is false; it fails only for p true and q false."],"x":["Simple statements are joined into compound statements by connectives. 'p and q' is the conjunction, written p ∧ q. 'p or q' is the disjunction, p ∨ q. 'If p then q' is the implication, p → q. 'p if and only if q' is the biconditional, p ↔ q.","When are they true? p ∧ q is true only when both p and q are true. p ∨ q is false only when both are false (it is an inclusive or: it is true if both are true). p → q is false only when p is true and q is false. p ↔ q is true when p and q have the same truth value.","Reading p → q: p is the hypothesis (condition) and q is the conclusion. The implication is true whenever p is false, whatever q is. This feels odd at first, but think of a promise: it is broken only when the condition happens and the result does not.","The words 'but', 'although' and 'also' usually behave like 'and'. 'Unless' behaves like 'or'. Do not confuse p → q with q → p: they are different statements."],"e":"p: 3 is odd (T). q: 4 is odd (F). Then p ∧ q is F, p ∨ q is T, p → q is F, q → p is T, and p ↔ q is F."},"1.3":{"s":"A truth table lists every combination of truth values of the simple statements and the resulting value of a compound statement. With n simple statements there are 2ⁿ rows. The negation of a statement has the opposite truth value. The negation of 'p and q' is 'not p or not q', and of 'p or q' is 'not p and not q' (De Morgan's laws).","w":["Negating 'and' gives 'or' (and the other way round), with each part negated."],"x":["A truth table lists every possible combination of truth values of the simple statements and the value of the compound statement in each row. With n simple statements there are 2ⁿ rows: 2 for one, 4 for two, 8 for three. List the rows in a fixed order, such as TT, TF, FT, FF, so that none is missed.","Build a table column by column: first the simple statements, then the smaller parts, then the whole statement in the last column. Work from the inside of the brackets outwards.","The negation of a statement p is written ~p and has the opposite truth value. De Morgan's laws: ~(p ∧ q) is ~p ∨ ~q, and ~(p ∨ q) is ~p ∧ ~q. Also ~(p → q) is p ∧ ~q, and ~(~p) is p. So the negation of 'and' gives 'or', and the other way round.","To negate a sentence in words, find its form first. The negation of 'If it rains, I stay at home' is 'It rains and I do not stay at home'. Common mistake: writing the negation of 'and' as '~p ∧ ~q'."],"e":"Truth table of p ∧ ~q. Rows (p, q): TT gives F, TF gives T, FT gives F, FF gives F. So the statement is true in only one row, p true and q false."},"1.4":{"s":"A statement pattern is a compound statement built from letters p, q, r and connectives. Two patterns are logically equivalent if their truth tables are identical in the last column. Useful equivalences include p → q ≡ ~p ∨ q, the contrapositive ~q → ~p, and the biconditional as two implications. The converse and inverse are not equivalent to the original implication.","w":["p → q is equivalent to its contrapositive ~q → ~p, not to its converse q → p."],"x":["A statement pattern is a compound statement built from letters such as p, q, r and connectives. Two patterns are logically equivalent, written ≡, when they have the same truth value in every row, that is, when the last columns of their truth tables are the same.","Equivalences to know: p → q ≡ ~p ∨ q. p ↔ q ≡ (p → q) ∧ (q → p). De Morgan's laws. Double negation ~(~p) ≡ p. Distributive laws: p ∧ (q ∨ r) ≡ (p ∧ q) ∨ (p ∧ r), and p ∨ (q ∧ r) ≡ (p ∨ q) ∧ (p ∨ r). Also p ∨ (p ∧ q) ≡ p.","From p → q we make three related statements. The converse is q → p. The inverse is ~p → ~q. The contrapositive is ~q → ~p. Only the contrapositive is equivalent to p → q. The converse and the inverse are equivalent to each other, but not to the original.","To prove an equivalence, either compare truth tables, or change one side step by step with the known laws. Mistake to avoid: assuming that the converse is also true."],"e":"If a number is divisible by 6, then it is divisible by 3. Converse: if divisible by 3, then divisible by 6 (false, 9 is a counter-example). Contrapositive: if not divisible by 3, then not divisible by 6 (true)."},"1.5":{"s":"A statement pattern is a tautology if it is true in every row of its truth table, a contradiction if it is false in every row, and a contingency if it is sometimes true and sometimes false. The negation of a tautology is a contradiction and the other way round.","w":["p ∨ ~p is a tautology; p ∧ ~p is a contradiction."],"x":["A statement pattern is a tautology if it is true in every row of its truth table, whatever the truth values of its simple statements. It is a contradiction if it is false in every row. It is a contingency if it is true in some rows and false in others.","Standard examples: p ∨ ~p is a tautology, and p ∧ ~p is a contradiction. p → p is a tautology. (p ∧ q) → p is a tautology. p → q is a contingency, since it is false only in the row p = T, q = F.","Relations: the negation of a tautology is a contradiction, and the negation of a contradiction is a tautology. The conjunction of a statement with a tautology has the same value as the statement itself. A disjunction with a tautology is a tautology.","To decide the type, either write the truth table, or look for a row that makes the pattern false (to test a tautology). One row that gives T and one that gives F proves that it is a contingency."],"e":"Test p → (q → p). It equals ~p ∨ (~q ∨ p) = (~p ∨ p) ∨ ~q. Since ~p ∨ p is always true, the whole statement is true in every row: a tautology."},"1.6":{"s":"A quantifier says how many objects a statement covers. The universal quantifier means 'for all' and the existential quantifier means 'there exists'. The negation of 'for all' is 'there exists one for which it fails', and the negation of 'there exists' is 'for all it fails'. The dual of a statement swaps and with or, and tautology with contradiction.","w":["Negating 'for every x' gives 'for some x … not', not 'for no x'."],"x":["A quantifier tells how many objects a statement is about. The universal quantifier 'for all' (∀) says that the property holds for every object. The existential quantifier 'there exists' (∃) says that it holds for at least one object. A sentence with a variable becomes a statement after a quantifier is put in front.","Negation: the negation of 'for all x, P(x)' is 'there exists x such that P(x) is false'. The negation of 'there exists x such that P(x)' is 'for all x, P(x) is false'. Negate the quantifier and also the property. One counter-example is enough to prove that a 'for all' statement is false.","Duality. The dual of a statement is formed by interchanging ∧ with ∨, and the tautology t with the contradiction c. The letters themselves are not negated. For example, the dual of p ∧ (q ∨ r) is p ∨ (q ∧ r). If two statements are equivalent, their duals are also equivalent.","Common mistakes: negating 'for all' as 'for none', and negating the letters when writing a dual."],"e":"Negate 'Every square has four equal sides'. It becomes 'There is a square that does not have four equal sides'. The negation of 'Some prime is even' is 'Every prime is odd'."},"1.7":{"s":"Switches in an electric circuit act like statements: a closed switch is true (1) and an open switch is false (0). Switches in series correspond to 'and' and switches in parallel to 'or'. A circuit's switching table lists whether current flows for each setting of the switches, so logic can be used to simplify circuits.","w":["Series = AND (both must be closed); parallel = OR (either may be closed)."],"x":["An electric switch can be treated as a statement. A closed switch (current can pass) is true, with value 1. An open switch is false, with value 0. The complement S′ of a switch S is open when S is closed, and closed when S is open, like the negation ~p.","Switches in series, p and q one after the other, let the current pass only if both are closed. This is p ∧ q. Switches in parallel, side by side, let the current pass if at least one is closed. This is p ∨ q.","A switching table lists the state of the circuit, 1 for current and 0 for none, for every setting of the switches, as a truth table does. The circuit with p in series with the parallel pair q, r is p ∧ (q ∨ r). A switch S in parallel with S′ always conducts: it is a tautology. A switch S in series with S′ never conducts: it is a contradiction.","To simplify a circuit, write its expression, simplify with the laws of logic, and draw the simpler circuit. Common mistake: mixing up series with 'or'. Series is always 'and'."],"e":"Circuit: p in series with (q parallel r). Expression p ∧ (q ∨ r). Current flows when p = 1 and at least one of q, r is 1: 3 of the 8 settings."}};
